The user wants to combine the functionality of the two selected widgets:
1. `shape:finder` - the Closest Sticky Note widget (fluid.demos.closestNoteFinder) - shows the closest sticky note to the widget
2. `shape:imagegen` - the Image Generator widget (fluid.demos.imageGenerator) - generates an image from a prompt

The combined functionality: "move it close to a sticky note and press a button to generate an image". So the idea is:
- A widget that finds the closest sticky note (like closestNoteFinder)
- And generates an image based on that note's text (like imageGenerator), presumably with a button press

So the workflow: user moves the combined widget next to a sticky note, presses a button, and the image generator searches Pixabay for the note's text.

Let me design an umbrella/composed widget. The strategy hints say:

"To compose two widgets, define a fresh 'umbrella' layer with `fluid.topLevelWidget` as the last of its `$layers`, holding each of them as a subcomponent by means of a `$component` record naming its existing layer. Choose the view of just one of them as the view of the umbrella with `view: "{first}.view"`, and cross-inject between them with references."

Hmm, but the combined widget should probably be a single cohesive widget with its own template, rather than two sub-widgets side by side. Let me think about what's the best UX: "move it close to a sticky note and press a button to generate an image". So the widget should:
- Show the closest note
- Have a button "Generate image" that uses the note's text as the prompt
- Show the generated image

Option A: Compose existing layers via umbrella. But the closestNoteFinder widget's UI is "Closest Sticky Note / <text> / Distance: N px" and the imageGenerator is a full input+buttons+image panel. Composing them with one's view would give a weird mix.

Option B: Write a fresh combined SFC layer that reuses logic: 
- Reuse the closest-note finding via computing it
- Button that sets state.prompt to the closest note's text and calls generate()
- Otherwise reuse the imageGenerator's generate logic.

Actually, we can define a fresh layer that includes both existing layers? The layers are SFCs defining `fluid.demos.closestNoteFinder` and `fluid.demos.imageGenerator`, both extending `fluid.topLevelWidget`. An umbrella could have `$layers: ["fluid.demos.closestNoteFinder", "fluid.demos.imageGenerator", "fluid.topLevelWidget"]`? Wait — combining layers via merge: both are top-level widgets with templates... Merging two SFC layers would merge their members. That could produce conflicts (both define `view`, both define `state`...). Actually the SFC loader adds `view` subcomponents to each layer; the merge would keep the view from whichever layer wins. The state definitions differ (closestNoteFinder has no `state` reactiveRoot but has `closest`, `noteText`; imageGenerator has `state` with prompt etc.). Merging might actually work: the child's `view` from imageGenerator could show its own template but references like `{self}.text` would need the closest note... but imageGenerator's template references `state.prompt` etc. The button label/generate logic would tie to `state.prompt`, not the note text. We'd want the button to set `state.prompt = noteText` first.

Hmm, but simpler and more robust: write a fresh combined layer as an SFC that:
- Extends `fluid.topLevelWidget` directly.
- Reuses `fluid.demos.closestNoteFinder.findClosest` function for computing closest note.
- Reuses `fluid.demos.imageGenerator`'s functions: `generate` works on a `state` object with prompt/isGenerating/error/images/index/refreshedId. We can reuse `fluid.demos.imageGenerator.generate`, `showAnother`, `refresh`, `currentImage`, `cannotGenerate`, `cannotShowAnother`, `buttonLabel` by giving our state the same shape. The state shape needs: prompt, isGenerating, error, images, index, refreshedId. 

- Button: "Generate image" which sets state.prompt to the closest note's text, then calls generate. Also handle the case of no note (closest.distance null or text like "No sticky notes found"/"Not placed on a canvas"): disable or show message.

Also the findClosest is in the closestNoteFinder layer which is already loaded in the workspace (it's defined in the workspace PTF). So we can reference `fluid.demos.closestNoteFinder.findClosest` as a global function. Similarly `fluid.demos.pixabay.search` is presumably loaded (their comment says "The search itself is in js/pixabay.js"). And `fluid.demos.imageGenerator.generate` etc. are defined in the workspace already since shape:imagegen is loaded.

So the fresh layer:

```js
fluid.def("fluid.demos.noteImageGenerator", {
    $layers: "fluid.topLevelWidget",
    preferredSize: {w: 380, h: 520},
    hostBounds: ??? 
```

Wait — how does closestNoteFinder get hostBounds? It's from `fluid.topLevelWidget`. Let me check: closestNoteFinder has `closest: "$compute:fluid.demos.closestNoteFinder.findClosest({tldrawHost}.notes, {self}.hostBounds)"` with a comment: "our own bounds, as received by fluid.topLevelWidget from our tldraw view". So `hostBounds` is provided by `fluid.topLevelWidget` layer. Good — we extend fluid.topLevelWidget too, so we can use the same compute.

Our combined layer could even subclass/reuse parts. Since closestNoteFinder is an SFC with its own template, we can't easily embed its subcomponents as members... Actually we CAN: the SFC defines members like `closest` computed, `noteText`, `distance`, `distanceLine` subcomponent. If we create an umbrella layer `$layers: ["fluid.demos.closestNoteFinder", "fluid.demos.noteImageGenerator", "fluid.topLevelWidget"]`, we'd merge in `closest` and `noteText` and `distanceLine` for free, and our own layer would add image generation. That could work and maximizes reuse.

But wait, there may be conflicts: closestNoteFinder defines `view` subcomponent (from its template). If noteImageGenerator also defines a `view` subcomponent, merge: `view` is a `$component` record in each layer; merging two `$component` records at `view`... The body of a `$component` record is a layer merged at subcomponent precedence. Hmm, two `$component` records at the same key — merge rules say plain objects merge recursively key by key, but a key holding a signal record ($component is a signal record) is replaced as a unit. So our layer's `view` would replace theirs. Fine.

But careful: the framework heuristics require `fluid.topLevelWidget` as the last of its `$layers` in our fresh layer's `$layers` (per the rules: "the top-level `$layers` of that layer's `fluid.def` must name `fluid.topLevelWidget` explicitly ... as the LAST of its layers"). So: `$layers: ["fluid.demos.closestNoteFinder", "fluid.demos.noteImageGenerator"]`? Wait, but the noteImageGenerator IS our layer currently being defined. Hmm, no — the rule applies to the layer whose document we're creating: for a fresh layer, name `fluid.topLevelWidget` last. If our fresh layer is `fluid.demos.noteImageGenerator` with `$layers: ["fluid.demos.closestNoteFinder"]`, then `fluid.topLevelWidget` comes before via inheritance... The rule as stated: "Whenever you define a layer in your document and construct a fresh or replacement widget from it, the top-level `$layers` of that layer's `fluid.def` must name `fluid.topLevelWidget` explicitly - even if it is already inherited from a parent layer - and as the LAST of its layers".

So our layer must be: `$layers: ["fluid.demos.closestNoteFinder", "fluid.topLevelWidget"]`. That inherits closing logic from closestNoteFinder, and names topLevelWidget last. 

What do we inherit from closestNoteFinder?
- `closest`, `noteText`, `distance`, `distanceLine` subcomponent — all reusable!
- Its `view` subcomponent (its own template) — will be replaced by our own `view` record (since our layer is stronger, its `$component` at `view` replaces).

But careful: closestNoteFinder's SFC loader added `view` as a `$component` with `$layers: "fluid.sfcTemplateViewComponent"`. Our layer can define `view: {$component: {$layers: "fluid.templateViewComponent", template: "..."}}`. Since our layer is stronger, our `$component` record replaces the weaker one as a unit. Good.

Then in our template we can show:
- Closest note text: `@{{closestNoteFinder}.closest.text}` — wait, scope: references in the template resolve against the owning component, and context names search by nicknames. The nicknames include layer names' last segment. The owning component has layers: fluid.demos.closestNoteFinder, fluid.topLevelWidget (and our nickname?). Context name "closest" is a member name (the computed member `closest`). Hmm — context name could be a member name of the component itself? "A member name, e.g. {panel}: A component of that member name, in scope." But `closest` is not a component, it's a computed value. Context names refer to components. However, for reading values, we can just use `@{closest.text}` (path in self) since `closest` is a member of self. That's simplest.

Actually, wait: is `closest` merged into our component? Merge: def precedence, closestNoteFinder contributes `closest` computed; our layer doesn't override it; yes, it stays.

Similarly, we can reuse the `distanceLine` subcomponent? That would render "Distance: N px" — we could include `<div @id="distanceLine"></div>` in our template. That works since the subcomponent exists as a member. Nice — maximal reuse.

Then add our image generation:

